This was my first Tourist ascension.  It started off smoothly; I got
two wishes before completing the Mines . . . one went to SDSM and the
other to an AoLS.  I found a bag of holding in the Mines, so Sokoban
wasn't necessary.

The first half of the game was pretty standard and nondescript . . .
the Oracle was very early (I think DL5), and the entrance to the Quest
was in the Big Room (DL14 or so).  There were no barracks, beehives,
zoos, leprechaun halls, Ludios, or anything else of interest, and only
one shop (armor) outside of Mine Town and the Quest.

The Quest was no major problem.  The Master of Thieves is really
pretty wimpy when you're wielding the +7 Mjollnir and have a low AC. 
Once I had the PYEC, I knew this was a strong ascension candidate.

I was doing very well by the time I reached the castle.  I'd already
managed to get my AC down to -51 through heavy enchantment, a Cloak of
Protection, and the use of a +5 Ring of Protection.  I used the
drawbridge to take care of all the big baddies, and walked in to
dispatch the rest.  I cleared the castle, and went to get the wand of
wishing.  Everything was going great.

That's when I made my first enormous mistake.  I'd been carrying a
wand of cancellation in a separate sack.  Just after getting the wand
of wishing, I tried to put something else in my bag of holding . . .
and accidentally hit the letter for my sack.  Uh-oh . . . there goes,
um, everything.  Many good rings, wands, scrolls, and potions, a
completely unused wand of wishing, and lots of other good junk.  I was
left with my armor, the PYEC, Mjollnir, and almost nothing else worth
mentioning.

When the accident happened, I was most concerned about the wand of
wishing.  My bigger problem, as it turns out, was actually the
candles.  Minetown only had 5 more, so I wasn't sure where I'd get the
other two.

I returned to my stash at DL1 to stock up with whatever I had left
there (which wasn't much).  The best things were my stacks of scrolls
(several identify, a taming, some enchantments, and miscellaneous
others).  Just as I opened my closet door, a red naga sneaked up
behind me and started breathing fire.  The fire missed, I saw a lot of
puffs of smoke, and the scrolls were ALL GONE (about 20 total).

I was feeling pretty despondent at this point, but figured if I'd been
able to collect all that stuff once, I could do it again.  I was
hoping to find some candles in Orcus Town.

Skip ahead about 20000 turns, and I'm at the bottom of Gehennom.  I've
marked the VS, killed all the major demons, and have the candelabrum
in hand, but still only five candles.  I went through a whole wand of
polymorph trying to get more (with all the recharges I could get with
the PYEC; probably a total of 50 polymorphs) but got none.

Now the plan was just to wander the dungeon until I either found
candles or a wish.  The only unexplored part of Gehennom was Rodney's
tower, so I figured I may as well clear it out.

Just outside of Rodney's chamber, the RNG decided it liked me a lot,
and an elf dropped a magic lamp.  I blessed it, used it, and got two
candles.  Things were looking up.  That's right when the game crashed.

I was able to use "recover" to get the game back, but of course
"recover" only rolls back to the last level change.  Which was before
I got the magic lamp.  Which was dropped by an elf.  Who was randomly
generated by a magic trap.  Things were looking bad again.

So, back to the "just find some candles" plan.  I was heading back up
through Gehennom, planning to ransack the main dungeon all over again,
when I found a bag of tricks.  I figured it would come in handy for a
sacrifest later, so I tucked it away . . . in my bag of holding. 
That's right, I did it again.  This time I lost, along with all my
regular good stuff, the remaining five candles and my only source of
levitation.  Things were looking worse than ever, but I was
determined.  I figured I'd just start behaving like an extinctionist
until I found seven more candles.

To add insult to injury, a giant I killed not 50 turns later dropped
two candles.

Skip ahead about 20000 more turns.  This is by far the longest game
I've ever played.  Suddenly I found two wands of polymorph, and
decided to have another poly party for candles.  This one was much
more successful . . . I got 9 (along with a sweet stack of 8 scrolls
of genocide).

A quick aside about protection: I'd bought, lost, and rebought all my
protection at least three times by this point.  All of the gold in the
entire dungeon was in the possession of the Priest of Moloch in the
VoD, and all the other priests were dead.  I had no good options for
buying my protection back (which I'd managed to lose again) . . .
until I found the rings of polymorph and poly control almost on top of
each other.  I polymorphed into a leprechaun, hit the priest once,
gained 96000 gold from that one hit, then used a magic harp to calm
him back down.  I was able to give that same gold back to the priest
to regain 10 points of protection . . . perfect.  Now I was really
ready to go the distance.

I figured this would be a good time to gain intrinsic teleport control
to free up a ring slot.  I started burning through cursed scrolls of
genocide and chowing down on tengu . . . it took 6 scrolls before I
finally felt "in control of myself".  I immediately level 'ported all
the way to the entrance to Rodney's tower.

I decided I'd try taking Rodney in melee, since I had strong weapons
(+7 Mojo, +7 silver saber, #twoweapon).  This was a mistake.  After
doubletroubling me, he (they) managed to 1) steal the PYEC, 2)
disintegrate my +5 fireproof speed boots, and 3) touch of death me
(thank you AoLS), all within two turns.  I decided instadeath was a
better tack for Rodney, and didn't make that mistake again.

After all I'd been through, the rest of the game was cake.  Conflict
helps in Moloch's Sanctum a lot.  On the way back, I went through a
series of pet saddled dragons (Luck, Wish, Joy, and finally Saffron). 
Saffron was with me all the way from mid-Gehennom to the Plane of Air.

The planes: Earth was easy as always, Air wasn't too bad thanks to my
magic harp, Fire was very easy (the portal was only about 8 squares
away), and Water was mildly tedious, but not too bad.

The Astral Plane . . . I was extremely well prepared, so I wasn't too
worried.  The first altar I went to was the correct one . . . but I
wasn't ready to end the game after fighting so hard for so long.  I
cleared the whole Astral Plane, leaving only Priests and Angels of The
Lady.  A couple of giants were nice enough to leave me some boulders,
so I jailed all three of the Riders together (see the map below).  It
was a lot of fun, but Death cost me about 200 max HP in the process. 
Once the level was satisfactorily under control, I sacrificed the
Amulet and the rest was history.

I thought the Tourist class was a little harder at the beginning, but
actually easier than pure fighters later on, since Tourists can become
decent spellcasters.  This game was great fun, and ascending after
117599 turns of perseverence is very rewarding.



